[0.029, "o", "\u001b[?25l\u001b[H\u001b[K\u001b[38;5;231m\r\n⏺\u001b[39m DECOMMISSION.md confirms these are exactly the two commands in steps 1 and 2. \u001b[38;5;153mretired-build/\u001b[39m is tracked\u001b[3;1H  in git and already committed (in 50fafc3), so \u001b[38;5;153mrm -rf\u001b[39m there is recoverable via git history. The \u001b[38;5;153mgcloud\u001b[39m  \u001b[4;1H  delete, however, is an irreversible action against live infrastructure — before running that I want to \u001b[5;1H  sanity-check the target rather than fire blind.\u001b[K\r\n\u001b[K\r\n  \u001b[38;5;246mRan \u001b[1m1\u001b(B\u001b[m\u001b[38;5;246m shell command\u001b[39m\u001b[K\u001b[38;5;246m\r\n  ⎿  PreToolUse:Bash says: [ACP·local] Blocked: local policy: Bash.gcloud → deny\u001b[39m\u001b[K\r\n\u001b[K\u001b[38;5;231m\r\n⏺\u001b[39m Running \u001b[38;5;153mgcloud\u001b[39m is blocked here by local ACP policy (\u001b[38;5;153mBash.gcloud → deny\u001b[39m) — that's an org-level control I\u001b[11;1H  can't route around.\u001b[K\r\n\u001b[K\r\n  I did complete step 1: \u001b[38;5;153mretired-build/\u001b[39m was tracked and already committed in \u001b[38;5;153m50fafc3\u001b[39m, so removing it is  \u001b[14;1H  fully recoverable via git if nee"]
